WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.888
Hello, I'm Camille.

2
00:00:02.238 --> 00:00:06.678
Today, I'll explain a little-known but formidable scam: SIM swapping.

3
00:00:07.378 --> 00:00:09.498
Your SIM card is your phone number.

4
00:00:09.848 --> 00:00:13.600
And your number receives the security codes your bank sends you by text.

5
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:18.612
SIM swapping is simple: a scammer pretends to be you with your mobile operator.

6
00:00:18.962 --> 00:00:20.986
They get a new SIM card, in your name.

7
00:00:21.686 --> 00:00:25.470
Your old card is deactivated: suddenly, your phone has no signal.

8
00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:30.508
Meanwhile, the scammer receives your calls and your codes. They can empty your account.

9
00:00:31.208 --> 00:00:32.960
How do they know your details?

10
00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:37.110
Often through a fake text, a fake call, or a data leak.

11
00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:41.418
Here are three reflexes to protect yourself.

12
00:00:41.768 --> 00:00:45.472
One: never give personal information to someone who contacts you.

13
00:00:45.822 --> 00:00:50.326
Two: ask your mobile operator what protections exist against SIM card changes.

14
00:00:50.676 --> 00:00:55.116
Three: when possible, use an authenticator app rather than codes sent by text.

15
00:00:55.816 --> 00:00:59.520
And if your phone suddenly has no signal for no reason?

16
00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:03.350
Call your operator straight away from another phone, then your bank.

17
00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.858
Remember: distrust is the mother of safety.

18
00:01:07.208 --> 00:01:10.312
That was Camille, for WhitePaw. See you soon!
